/**
 * @module wire
 *
 * Wire, an HTML template engine.
 *
 * A Wire template is an ordinary HTML document. The source is parsed by
 * the same [WHATWG](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/) parser
 * that the `html` module uses, and any valid HTML5 document is already
 * a valid Wire template. Wire adds attributes for ordinary elements,
 * and values written between `{{` and `}}`.
 *
 * Because Wire parses templates instead of substituting text into them,
 * it knows that one value is inside a paragraph, another inside an
 * `href` and another inside a `<script>`, and it escapes each one for
 * where it actually is. A value can never turn into markup.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import wire
 *
 * echo wire.render_string(
 *   '<p>Hello {{ name }}</p>',
 *   { name: '<b>Ada</b>' }
 * )
 * # <p>Hello &lt;b&gt;Ada&lt;/b&gt;</p>
 * ```
 *
 * ### Templates from files
 *
 * `render_string()` is handy for a quick test and for examples like the
 * ones here. Real templates are kept in files under a root directory,
 * which is `templates` in the working directory unless you choose a
 * different one:
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import wire
 *
 * var view = wire()
 * view.set_root('./views')
 *
 * echo view.render('pages/home', { user })
 * ```
 *
 * If the path as written doesn't name a file, `.html` is added, so
 * `'pages/home'` finds `pages/home.html`. Use `set_extension()` to
 * change the extension.
 *
 * Every path resolves **inside** the root and nowhere else. A path that
 * climbs out with `..`, or an absolute path somewhere else, is refused.
 * This matters as soon as a path is built from anything a request
 * supplied.
 *
 * ### Values
 *
 * Anything between `{{` and `}}` is an expression. `wire.expression`
 * describes the full language, but in practice it works the way you'd
 * expect:
 *
 * ```wire
 * <h1>{{ post.title }}</h1>
 * <p>{{ post.author.name|upper }}</p>
 * <p>{{ post.tags|join(', ') }}</p>
 * <p>{{ post.views > 1000 ? 'popular' : 'quiet' }}</p>
 * <p>{{ user.nickname ?? user.name }}</p>
 * ```
 *
 * A name that was never supplied is `nil`, and so is any key read from
 * it, so on a request with no user, `{{ user.address.city }}` renders
 * as nothing instead of failing. Use `x-if` when the difference
 * matters.
 *
 * To write `{{` literally, write `%{{`.
 *
 * #### Filters
 *
 * A `|` followed by a name passes the value through a filter. Filters
 * can be chained, and they can take arguments:
 *
 * ```wire
 * {{ name|trim|title }}
 * {{ total|number_format(2) }}
 * {{ summary|truncate(80, '…') }}
 * ```
 *
 * `wire.filters` lists the forty or so filters that come with Wire, and
 * `register_filter()` adds your own.
 *
 * ### Escaping
 *
 * Every value is escaped for the place where it's written:
 *
 * | Where it is             | What happens                              |
 * | ----------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
 * | text between tags       | `&`, `<` and `>` become references         |
 * | an attribute value      | `&` and `"` become references              |
 * | `href`, `src`, `action` | the URL's scheme is checked as well        |
 * | `<script>`, `onclick`   | the value is encoded as JSON               |
 * | `<style>`               | anything outside a CSS-safe set is dropped |
 *
 * A `javascript:` URL in an `href` becomes `about:blank`. If an
 * application really needs another scheme, add it to the allowed list
 * with `set_url_schemes()`.
 *
 * Inside a `<script>` element, the value includes its own quotes, so
 * don't add any:
 *
 * ```wire
 * <script>
 *   var user = {{ name }};    // right, renders as "Ada"
 *   var user = '{{ name }}';  // wrong, renders as '"Ada"'
 * </script>
 * ```
 *
 * To have markup that you built yourself rendered as markup, pass it
 * through the `raw` filter, or wrap it in `wire.safe()` before you pass
 * it in:
 *
 * ```wire
 * <div class="body">{{ article.rendered_html|raw }}</div>
 * ```
 *
 * Using `raw` is a promise that the value is safe where it's written.
 * Using it on anything a user supplied is how a template ends up with a
 * cross-site scripting hole.
 *
 * Escaping can't be turned off. It can only be skipped for a particular
 * value, with `raw` or `wire.safe()`.
 *
 * ### Directives
 *
 * All of Wire's own attributes start with `x-`. Wire reserves that
 * prefix completely: an `x-` attribute that it doesn't recognize is an
 * error, so a typo like `x-fi` is caught the first time the template is
 * compiled.
 *
 * A directive can be used on any element, and it controls that element
 * as well as its contents. To get the effect of a directive without an
 * element in the output, put it on a `<template>` element, which Wire
 * removes. `<template>` is the only element that HTML leaves alone
 * everywhere, including inside `<head>`, a table or a `<select>`.
 *
 * #### Conditions
 *
 * ```wire
 * <p x-if="user.is_admin">Admin</p>
 * <p x-elif="user.is_staff">Staff</p>
 * <p x-else>Member</p>
 * ```
 *
 * `x-elif` and `x-else` attach to the sibling element just before them,
 * with only whitespace and comments allowed in between. `x-not` is the
 * opposite of `x-if`, and it can't be followed by `x-elif` or `x-else`.
 *
 * #### Loops
 *
 * ```wire
 * <li x-for="items" x-value="item" x-key="index">
 *   {{ index }}: {{ item.name }}
 * </li>
 * ```
 *
 * The element itself is repeated, not just its children. You can loop
 * over lists, dictionaries, strings and ranges. For a list, `x-key` is
 * set to the position, and for a dictionary, it's set to the key.
 *
 * Each iteration also sets a `loop` variable:
 *
 * | Field                | Value                                  |
 * | -------------------- | -------------------------------------- |
 * | `loop.index`         | the position, counting from 1          |
 * | `loop.index0`        | the position, counting from 0          |
 * | `loop.first`         | true on the first iteration            |
 * | `loop.last`          | true on the last iteration             |
 * | `loop.length`        | the number of entries                  |
 * | `loop.even`, `.odd`  | whether the position is even or odd    |
 * | `loop.key`, `.value` | the current entry, whether or not it's bound to a name |
 * | `loop.parent`        | the `loop` variable of the enclosing loop |
 *
 * `x-loop="row"` gives it a different name, so that two nested loops
 * can both be read without going through `parent`.
 *
 * An `x-if` on a repeated element is evaluated once for each iteration,
 * so it can use the loop's variables. In that position, it can't be
 * followed by `x-elif`.
 *
 * #### Content
 *
 * ```wire
 * <p x-text="summary"></p>
 * <div x-html="article.body|raw"></div>
 * <a x-attr="{ href: post.url, rel: post.external ? 'nofollow' : nil }">…</a>
 * ```
 *
 * `x-text` and `x-html` replace an element's children. `x-attr` adds
 * the entries of a dictionary to the element as attributes. A value of
 * `true` gives an attribute without a value, and `false` or `nil`
 * leaves the attribute out.
 *
 * ### Composition
 *
 * #### Including
 *
 * ```wire
 * <include path="partials/header.html" />
 * <template x-include="partials/header.html"></template>
 * ```
 *
 * These two lines do the same thing. `<include>`, `<extend>`,
 * `<declare>`, `<define>` and `<super>` are shorthand. They're
 * rewritten into the directive form while the source is tokenized,
 * which is why they work inside `<head>` or a table, where an unknown
 * element would be moved or dropped.
 *
 * A path is read as text, not as an expression, so you can write it
 * plainly and insert values where you need them:
 *
 * ```wire
 * <include path="themes/{{ theme }}/header.html" />
 * ```
 *
 * An included template can see the variables around it. `x-with` adds
 * variables, and `x-only`, written `only` on `<include>`, hides
 * everything else. Together they let you treat a partial as a component
 * with a defined interface:
 *
 * ```wire
 * <include path="components/badge" x-with="{ label: 'New', tone: 'green' }" only />
 * ```
 *
 * Anything written inside an include is passed to it as the region
 * named `content`, so a partial can wrap whatever it was given:
 *
 * ```wire
 * <!-- components/card.html -->
 * <div class="card">
 *   <h3>{{ title }}</h3>
 *   <declare name="content"></declare>
 * </div>
 *
 * <!-- the page -->
 * <include path="components/card" x-with="{ title: 'Totals' }">
 *   <p>{{ orders|length }} orders</p>
 * </include>
 * ```
 *
 * #### Inheritance
 *
 * A base template marks the regions that a page can replace:
 *
 * ```wire
 * <!-- layouts/page.html -->
 * <!DOCTYPE html>
 * <html>
 *   <head>
 *     <title>{{ title }}</title>
 *     <declare name="head"></declare>
 *   </head>
 *   <body>
 *     <main>
 *       <declare name="content"></declare>
 *     </main>
 *     <footer>
 *       <declare name="footer">
 *         <p>&copy; {{ year }}</p>
 *       </declare>
 *     </footer>
 *   </body>
 * </html>
 * ```
 *
 * A page fills them in:
 *
 * ```wire
 * <!-- pages/home.html -->
 * <extend base="layouts/page.html">
 *   <define name="content">
 *     <h1>Hello {{ user.name }}</h1>
 *   </define>
 *   <define name="footer">
 *     <super />
 *     <p>All rights reserved.</p>
 *   </define>
 * </extend>
 * ```
 *
 * A region that no template defines renders whatever the base template
 * put inside it. `<super />` renders the definition that the current
 * one replaces, so a page can add to a footer instead of repeating it.
 * Inheritance can be nested as deeply as you like, and a template in
 * the middle can declare regions of its own.
 *
 * A template can only extend one base, and everything in an extending
 * template has to be inside a `<define>`, because the base template
 * decides the structure. To define the same region twice, you have to
 * say so explicitly with `override`:
 *
 * ```wire
 * <define name="content" override>…</define>
 * ```
 *
 * ### Extending Wire from Zuri
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import wire
 *
 * var view = wire()
 *
 * # A filter: the value comes first, then whatever the template passed.
 * view.register_filter('excerpt', @(value, words) {
 *   var count = words == nil ? 25 : words
 *   return ' '.join(value.split('/\\s+/').take(count)) + '…'
 * })
 *
 * # A value that every template can use without being passed it.
 * view.register_global('site_name', 'Example')
 *
 * # A function that templates can call.
 * view.register_global('route', @(name) {
 *   return '/' + name
 * })
 * ```
 *
 * Used like this:
 *
 * ```wire
 * <p>{{ post.body|excerpt(40) }}</p>
 * <a href="{{ route('about') }}">{{ site_name }}</a>
 * ```
 *
 * `register_element()` takes over a tag name completely and passes
 * every element with that name to a function. Use it for anything that
 * the directives can't express.
 *
 * ### Compiling and caching
 *
 * A template is parsed once, not every time it's rendered. `render()`
 * compiles a template the first time it's used and keeps the result,
 * checking the file's modification time and size before using it again.
 *
 * That check costs one `stat` call per template per render. That's what
 * you want while you're writing templates, but not under load.
 * `set_auto_reload(false)` turns it off, and a long-running process can
 * call `clear_cache()` to pick up a new deployment.
 *
 * ### Comments
 *
 * HTML comments are treated as notes for the server, and they aren't
 * included in the page:
 *
 * ```wire
 * <!-- This never ships, and neither does {{ secret }}. -->
 * ```
 *
 * Nothing inside a comment is evaluated. Call `set_comments(true)` to
 * keep comments when they really are meant for the browser, such as
 * conditional comments or build markers.
 *
 * ### Errors
 *
 * Everything that Wire raises is a `WireError`, which records the
 * template and the line and column of the tag at fault.
 * `TemplateSyntaxError` comes from compiling, `RenderError` from
 * rendering, and `TemplateNotFoundError` from a path that doesn't lead
 * to a template.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * catch {
 *   echo view.render('pages/home', { user })
 * } as e {
 *   if instance_of(e, wire.WireError) {
 *     echo 'template ${e.location()}: ${e.reason}'
 *   }
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * @copyright 2026, Richard Ore and The Zuri Contributors
 */

import os

import @.values { Safe, safe, is_safe, stringify, truthy }
import @.errors {
  WireError,
  TemplateNotFoundError,
  TemplateSyntaxError,
  RenderError,
}

import @.constants
import @.escape
import @.expression
import @.filters
import @.normalize
import @.compile as _compile
import @.loader as _loader
import @.render as _render

/**
 * A template engine: a root directory, a set of filters, and a cache of
 * everything compiled so far.
 *
 * One of these is normally built at startup, configured once, and used
 * for the life of the process. Rendering does not change it, so it is
 * safe to render from several places at once.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import wire
 *
 * var view = wire()
 * view.set_root('./views')
 * view.register_global('site_name', 'Example')
 *
 * echo view.render('pages/home', { user })
 * ```
 */
class Wire {

  /**
   * Values that every template can read without being passed them.
   *
   * A variable with the same name passed to `render()` takes priority,
   * so a global is a default, not an override.
   */
  var globals = {}

  /**
   * The URL schemes allowed in an `href` and its relatives. Anything
   * else is replaced with `about:blank`.
   */
  var url_schemes = []

  /**
   * The directory template paths resolve inside.
   */
  var _root = ''

  /**
   * The extension tried when a path names no file as written.
   */
  var _extension = constants.DEFAULT_EXTENSION

  /**
   * The filters templates can use, keyed by name.
   */
  var _filters = {}

  /**
   * The tag names handed to a function instead of being written out.
   */
  var _elements = {}

  /**
   * Compiled templates, keyed by the file and the context they were
   * compiled for.
   */
  var _cache = {}

  /**
   * Whether a cached template is checked against the file before reuse.
   */
  var _auto_reload = true

  /**
   * Whether whitespace-only text is dropped from the output.
   */
  var _compact = false

  /**
   * Whether HTML comments reach the output.
   */
  var _comments = false

  /**
   * The loader, rebuilt whenever the root or the extension changes.
   */
  var _loader_instance = nil

  /**
   * @param ?dict options: Any of `root`, `extension`, `compact`,
   *    `comments`, `auto_reload` and `url_schemes`, each the same as the
   *    matching `set_` method.
   * @constructor
   * @throws ArgumentError when an option is not of the type it should
   *    be.
   */
  @new(options: ?dict) {
    self.globals = {}
    self.url_schemes = escape.DEFAULT_URL_SCHEMES.clone()

    self._root = os.join_paths(os.cwd(), constants.DEFAULT_ROOT)
    self._extension = constants.DEFAULT_EXTENSION
    self._filters = filters.BUILTIN.clone()
    self._elements = {}
    self._cache = {}
    self._auto_reload = true
    self._compact = false
    self._comments = false
    self._loader_instance = nil

    if options == nil {
      return
    }

    if !is_dict(options) {
      raise ArgumentError('dictionary expected in argument 1 (options)')
    }

    if options.contains('root') {
      self.set_root(options.root)
    }

    if options.contains('extension') {
      self.set_extension(options.extension)
    }

    if options.contains('compact') {
      self.set_compact(options.compact)
    }

    if options.contains('comments') {
      self.set_comments(options.comments)
    }

    if options.contains('auto_reload') {
      self.set_auto_reload(options.auto_reload)
    }

    if options.contains('url_schemes') {
      self.set_url_schemes(options.url_schemes)
    }
  }

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Configuration
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------

  /**
   * Sets the directory that template paths resolve inside.
   *
   * A relative path is taken from the working directory. The directory
   * doesn't have to exist yet. If it doesn't, a path under it is
   * reported as not found, like any other missing template.
   *
   * Changing the root empties the cache, because the same path might
   * now refer to a different file.
   *
   * @param string path
   * @returns Wire
   * @throws ArgumentError
   */
  set_root(path: string) {
    self._root = os.abs_path(path)
    self._loader_instance = nil
    self.clear_cache()

    return self
  }

  /**
   * The directory template paths resolve inside, as an absolute path.
   *
   * @returns string
   */
  root() {
    return self._root
  }

  /**
   * Creates the root directory if it doesn't exist, and returns whether
   * it had to.
   *
   * Wire never does this by itself. Creating directories isn't a
   * template engine's job, and a typo in the root should show up as a
   * missing template, not quietly create an empty directory.
   *
   * @returns bool
   * @throws Error if the directory can't be created
   */
  create_root() {
    if os.dir_exists(self._root) {
      return false
    }

    os.create_dir(self._root)
    return true
  }

  /**
   * Sets the extension tried when a path names no file as written.
   *
   * It has to begin with a dot. `render('home')` then looks for `home`
   * and then for `home` plus this.
   *
   * @param string extension
   * @returns Wire
   * @throws ArgumentError when it does not begin with a dot.
   */
  set_extension(extension: string) {
    if !extension.starts_with('.') {
      raise ArgumentError('a template extension has to begin with "."')
    }

    self._extension = extension
    self._loader_instance = nil
    self.clear_cache()

    return self
  }

  /**
   * Leaves text that's only whitespace out of the output when _compact_
   * is true.
   *
   * This is a simple kind of minification: the indentation between tags
   * is removed, and nothing else is changed. Whitespace inside a
   * `<pre>` element counts as whitespace-only when the whole run of
   * text is, so this can still change how preformatted text looks.
   * Leave it off where that matters, and use `html.minify()` instead,
   * which knows the difference.
   *
   * @param bool compact
   * @returns Wire
   * @throws ArgumentError
   */
  set_compact(compact: bool) {
    self._compact = compact
    self.clear_cache()

    return self
  }

  /**
   * Keeps HTML comments in the output when _comments_ is true.
   *
   * Comments are left out by default, so an HTML comment in a template
   * is a note that never reaches the browser. Nothing inside a comment
   * is evaluated either way.
   *
   * @param bool comments
   * @returns Wire
   * @throws ArgumentError
   */
  set_comments(comments: bool) {
    self._comments = comments
    self.clear_cache()

    return self
  }

  /**
   * Checks a cached template against its file before reusing it when
   * _reload_ is true, which is the default.
   *
   * The check is one `stat` per template per render. Leave it on while
   * templates are being written and turn it off where throughput
   * matters, remembering that `clear_cache()` is then the only way a
   * running process notices a deployment.
   *
   * @param bool reload
   * @returns Wire
   * @throws ArgumentError
   */
  set_auto_reload(reload: bool) {
    self._auto_reload = reload
    return self
  }

  /**
   * Replaces the list of URL schemes allowed in `href` and similar
   * attributes.
   *
   * A URL without a scheme is always allowed, so relative links keep
   * working whatever this is set to. A URL whose scheme isn't in the
   * list is replaced with `about:blank`.
   *
   * The default list is `escape.DEFAULT_URL_SCHEMES`. It intentionally
   * leaves out `javascript`, `vbscript`, `data` and `file`.
   *
   * ```zuri
   * view.set_url_schemes(escape.DEFAULT_URL_SCHEMES + ['app'])
   * ```
   *
   * @param list schemes: scheme names in lower case, without the colon
   * @returns Wire
   * @throws ArgumentError
   */
  set_url_schemes(schemes: list) {
    var out = []

    for scheme in schemes {
      if !is_string(scheme) {
        raise ArgumentError('a URL scheme has to be a string')
      }

      out.append(scheme.lower())
    }

    self.url_schemes = out
    return self
  }

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Registration
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------

  /**
   * Registers a filter that templates can use after a `|`.
   *
   * The value being filtered is passed as the first argument, followed
   * by the arguments the template passed. An argument that the template
   * left out is passed as `nil`, so give each parameter a default
   * instead of requiring it.
   *
   * Registering a name that already exists replaces the existing
   * filter, so an application can give `date` a meaning of its own.
   *
   * ```zuri
   * view.register_filter('excerpt', @(value, words) {
   *   var count = words == nil ? 25 : words
   *   return ' '.join(value.split('/\\s+/').take(count)) + '…'
   * })
   * ```
   *
   * If a filter returns a plain string, the result is escaped like any
   * other value. A filter that really does produce markup should return
   * `wire.safe()`, and it's then responsible for what's inside it.
   *
   * @param string name
   * @param callable handler
   * @returns Wire
   * @throws ArgumentError
   */
  register_filter(name: string, handler) {
    self._check_name('filter', name)

    if !is_callable(handler) {
      raise ArgumentError('a filter has to be callable')
    }

    self._filters[name] = handler
    return self
  }

  /**
   * Takes over a tag name, so that every element with that name is
   * passed to _handler_ instead of being written out.
   *
   * The handler is called with the `Wire` instance and a dictionary
   * describing the element:
   *
   * | Key          | Contains                                        |
   * | ------------ | ----------------------------------------------- |
   * | `name`       | the tag name                                    |
   * | `attributes` | its attributes, with every value already rendered and escaped |
   * | `content`    | its children, already rendered                  |
   * | `variables`  | the scope it was written in                     |
   *
   * Return `wire.safe(markup)` to write markup, any other value to
   * write it as escaped text, or `nil` to write nothing.
   *
   * ```zuri
   * view.register_element('icon', @(view, element) {
   *   var name = element.attributes.get('name', 'dot')
   *   return wire.safe('<svg class="icon"><use href="#${name}"></use></svg>')
   * })
   * ```
   *
   * Directives still work on an element that's been taken over, so
   * `<icon x-if="…" />` does what it looks like it does. Only use this
   * where the directives really can't express what you need. A partial
   * with `x-include` is easier to read, and you don't need to read Zuri
   * code to follow it.
   *
   * @param string name
   * @param callable handler
   * @returns Wire
   * @throws ArgumentError
   */
  register_element(name: string, handler) {
    self._check_name('element', name)

    if !is_callable(handler) {
      raise ArgumentError('an element handler has to be callable')
    }

    self._elements[name.lower()] = handler
    return self
  }

  /**
   * Makes _value_ available to every template as _name_.
   *
   * A variable with the same name passed to `render()` takes priority,
   * so a global is a default, not an override. A global that can be
   * called is a function that templates can call.
   *
   * @param string name
   * @param any value
   * @returns Wire
   * @throws ArgumentError
   */
  register_global(name: string, value) {
    self._check_name('global', name)

    self.globals[name] = value
    return self
  }

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Rendering
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------

  /**
   * Renders the template at _path_ and returns the page.
   *
   * _path_ is relative to the root and may leave off the extension.
   * _variables_ is what the template can read, and defaults to nothing
   * at all.
   *
   * ```zuri
   * echo view.render('pages/home', { user, posts })
   * ```
   *
   * @param string path
   * @param ?dict variables
   * @returns string
   * @throws TemplateNotFoundError when the template does not exist or is
   *    outside the root.
   * @throws TemplateSyntaxError when it does not compile.
   * @throws RenderError when rendering it fails.
   */
  render(path: string, variables: ?dict) {
    var template = self.load(path, nil, nil, 0, 0)
    return _render.Renderer(self).run(template, _variables(variables))
  }

  /**
   * Renders _source_ as a template and returns the page.
   *
   * _path_ names the source in any error raised and defaults to
   * `<source>`. Includes and extends inside the source still resolve
   * against the root.
   *
   * The result is not cached, since there is no file to key it on. Use
   * `render()` for anything rendered more than once.
   *
   * ```zuri
   * echo view.render_string('<p>{{ greeting }}</p>', { greeting: 'Hi' })
   * ```
   *
   * @param string source
   * @param ?dict variables
   * @param ?string path
   * @returns string
   * @throws TemplateSyntaxError
   * @throws RenderError
   */
  render_string(source: string, variables: ?dict, path: ?string) {
    var name = '<source>'
    if path != nil {
      name = path
    }

    var template = _compile.compile(source, name, nil, self._options())
    return _render.Renderer(self).run(template, _variables(variables))
  }

  /**
   * Compiles the template at _path_ without rendering it, and returns
   * it.
   *
   * This is useful for checking a directory of templates when a program
   * is built or starts, so that a syntax error is found then, instead
   * of on the request that reaches it.
   *
   * ```zuri
   * for name in os.list_dir('./views') {
   *   view.compile(name)
   * }
   * ```
   *
   * @param string path
   * @returns Template
   * @throws TemplateNotFoundError
   * @throws TemplateSyntaxError
   */
  compile(path: string) {
    return self.load(path, nil, nil, 0, 0)
  }

  /**
   * Forgets every compiled template.
   *
   * A long-running process with `set_auto_reload(false)` needs this to
   * notice a deployment.
   *
   * @returns Wire
   */
  clear_cache() {
    self._cache = {}
    return self
  }

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Used by the renderer
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------

  /**
   * The compiled template at _path_, from the cache if it's there and
   * still up to date.
   *
   * _context_ names the element the template will be placed inside, so
   * that a partial containing table rows is parsed knowing that it's
   * going into a table. _from_, _line_ and _column_ describe where the
   * include was written, for the error message if the path doesn't lead
   * to a template.
   *
   * The renderer calls this, and there's rarely a reason to call it
   * directly.
   *
   * @param string path
   * @param ?string context
   * @param ?string from
   * @param ?number line
   * @param ?number column
   * @returns Template
   * @throws TemplateNotFoundError
   * @throws TemplateSyntaxError
   */
  load(path: string, context, from: ?string, line: ?number, column: ?number) {
    var full = self._loader().resolve(path, from, line, column)
    var key = '${context}\n${full}'
    var cached = self._cache.get(key, nil)

    if cached != nil {
      if !self._auto_reload {
        return cached.template
      }

      if cached.fingerprint != nil and
          cached.fingerprint == self._loader().fingerprint(full) {
        return cached.template
      }
    }

    # Taken before the read, so a file rewritten between the two is seen
    # as stale next time rather than being cached as though it were the
    # version that was read.
    var fingerprint = self._loader().fingerprint(full)
    var source = self._loader().read(full)

    var template = _compile.compile(source, full, context, self._options())
    template.fingerprint = fingerprint

    self._cache[key] = { template, fingerprint }
    return template
  }

  /**
   * The filter called _name_, or `nil`.
   *
   * @param string name
   * @returns ?callable
   */
  filter(name: string) {
    return self._filters.get(name, nil)
  }

  /**
   * The handler registered for the tag _name_, or `nil`.
   *
   * @param string name
   * @returns ?callable
   */
  element(name: string) {
    if self._elements.is_empty() {
      return nil
    }

    return self._elements.get(name, nil)
  }

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Internals
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------

  /**
   * The loader, built on demand so that changing the root or the
   * extension is just a matter of dropping the old one.
   *
   * @returns Loader
   */
  _loader() {
    if self._loader_instance == nil {
      self._loader_instance = _loader.Loader(self._root, self._extension)
    }

    return self._loader_instance
  }

  /**
   * The options the compiler needs.
   *
   * @returns dict
   */
  _options() {
    return { compact: self._compact, comments: self._comments }
  }

  /**
   * Rejects a name that could never be written in a template.
   *
   * @param string kind
   * @param string name
   * @throws ArgumentError
   */
  _check_name(kind: string, name: string) {
    if name.match('/^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_\\-]*$/') == false {
      raise ArgumentError(
        'a ${kind} name has to be a plain name, not "${name}"',
      )
    }

    if name.starts_with('_') {
      raise ArgumentError(
        'a ${kind} name cannot start with an underscore, since a template ' +
            'cannot read one',
      )
    }
  }
}

/**
 * A new `Wire`.
 *
 * _options_ takes any of `root`, `extension`, `compact`, `comments`,
 * `auto_reload` and `url_schemes`, each the same as the matching `set_`
 * method, so a whole configuration can be written in one place.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import wire
 *
 * var view = wire({
 *   root: './views',
 *   auto_reload: false,
 * })
 * ```
 *
 * @param ?dict options
 * @returns Wire
 * @default
 */
def wire(options: ?dict) {
  return Wire(options)
}

/**
 * The `Wire` the module-level `render()` and `render_string()` use.
 */
var _shared = Wire()

/**
 * The `Wire` instance used by the module-level `render()` and
 * `render_string()` functions.
 *
 * Configure it to use those functions without creating a `Wire` of your
 * own. That's fine for a small program, but anything that needs two
 * different roots should create its own instances.
 *
 * ```zuri
 * import wire
 *
 * wire.shared().set_root('./views')
 * echo wire.render('pages/home', { user })
 * ```
 *
 * @returns Wire
 */
def shared() {
  return _shared
}

/**
 * Renders the template at _path_ using the shared `Wire`.
 *
 * @param string path
 * @param ?dict variables
 * @returns string
 * @throws WireError
 */
def render(path: string, variables: ?dict) {
  return _shared.render(path, variables)
}

/**
 * Renders _source_ using the shared `Wire`.
 *
 * @param string source
 * @param ?dict variables
 * @param ?string path
 * @returns string
 * @throws WireError
 */
def render_string(source: string, variables: ?dict, path: ?string) {
  return _shared.render_string(source, variables, path)
}

/**
 * The variables a render was given, defaulting to none.
 *
 * @param ?dict variables
 * @returns dict
 * @throws ArgumentError
 */
def _variables(variables: ?dict) {
  if variables == nil {
    return {}
  }

  return variables.clone()
}
